The gain setting is logged with every sample, so the true AC and DC can be recovered for SpO2.
Samples at the ADC limits are flagged as clipped and excluded. 


Mechanical Design:
Reflectance layout: LEDs and photodiodes on the same face, a few mm apart, with an opaque barrier between them.  
Opaque housing and consistent contact pressure (strap or clip)

Limitations:
Known limitation: the AC (heartbeat) part is only about 0.5–2% of the DC level, and there is no DC subtraction or second gain stage before the ADC. The 12-bit ADC (≈ 0.61 mV per step) has to measure the full signal, DC included, so small changes in the AC fall through the cracks between steps and get rounded away (quantization error). Averaging several ADC readings per slot partly reduces this. The planned fix is an optical AFE (analog front-end) IC in the wearable version, which removes the DC and amplifies the AC before converting it.

